    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 2 (Questions 7&ndash;12)</div>\n<p>In the taxonomy of narrative theory, Wayne C. Booth&rsquo;s introduction of the &ldquo;unreliable narrator&rdquo; in <i>The Rhetoric of Fiction</i> (1961) demarcated a crucial conceptual paradigm shift: the speaker of a literary text cannot be unproblematically conflated with the implicit authorial consciousness orchestrating the work. An unreliable narrator is structurally defined by an epistemic dissonance&mdash;a persistent divergence between the narrator&rsquo;s subjective distortions, cognitive delusions, or self-serving rationalizations and the objective reality reconstructed by the perceptive reader. Booth established that such unreliability emerges along multiple axes, including moral corruption, intellectual limitation, and temporal distance. While conventional narrative unreliability often hinges on naive moral innocence or limited childhood perception, Vladimir Nabokov&rsquo;s 1962 masterpiece <i>Pale Fire</i> elevates cognitive unreliability into an intricate ontological labyrinth, interrogating the fragile boundaries separating artistic creation, critical interpretation, and psychiatric madness.</p>\n<p>Structurally masquerading as an academic critical edition, <i>Pale Fire</i> consists of a 999-line autobiographical poem in heroic couplets composed by the eminent American poet John Shade, enveloped by a foreword, expansive critical commentary, and an index authored by his self-proclaimed editor, Charles Kinbote. Where Shade&rsquo;s poem is an intimate, contemplative meditation on domestic love, mortality, and the tragic suicide of his daughter Hazel, Kinbote&rsquo;s commentary violently hijacks Shade&rsquo;s verses. Throughout the writing process, Kinbote had pestered Shade with tales of European royalty, eagerly expecting the American poet to immortalize them in verse. In an astonishing display of megalomaniacal hermeneutics, Kinbote insists that Shade&rsquo;s quiet domestic poetry is actually an allegorical chronicle of the deposed King Charles II (&ldquo;the Beloved&rdquo;) of Zembla, a mythical, distant Northern kingdom from which Kinbote claims to have fled following an anti-monarchical revolution.</p>\n<p>The dramatic irony animating <i>Pale Fire</i> relies on the reader&rsquo;s bifurcated vision. As Kinbote annotates Shade&rsquo;s solemn lines with increasingly absurd anecdotes of royal Zemblan intrigue, homosexual trysts, and theatrical escapes, the reader gradually deciphers the tragic reality concealed behind Kinbote&rsquo;s grandiose delusions. Clues scattered across the apparatus reveal that Kinbote is not a displaced monarch at all, but rather Dr. V. Botkin, an eccentric, deeply isolated, and psychotically depressed Russian emigr&eacute; academic teaching at the same college. The poignant tragedy of the narrative crystallizes in the fatal misunderstanding at its climax: Shade is inadvertently shot and killed not by a relentless Zemblan regicide assassin named Jakob Gradus (as Kinbote passionately insists), but by an escaped mental asylum patient named Jack Grey, who was seeking vengeance against the judge whose house Kinbote was subletting. Kinbote&rsquo;s elaborate critical apparatus thus emerges as a desperate psychological fortress designed to confer cosmic destiny and heroic meaning upon a random, absurd misfortune.</p>\n<p>Beyond its psychological brilliance, Nabokov deploys Kinbote&rsquo;s erratic scholarship to mount a devastating satirical critique of aggressive literary criticism. Kinbote epitomizes the parasitic critic who refuses to submit to the sovereign artistry of the primary text, instead forcibly conscripting the author&rsquo;s work into the service of his own narcissistic obsessions. Yet <i>Pale Fire</i> refuses simplistic resolution. Literary scholars have engaged in protracted debates over whether the entire apparatus might have been authored by Shade himself inventing Kinbote, or whether Nabokov engineered an infinite hermeneutic hall of mirrors devoid of an absolute ground. In doing so, Nabokov demonstrates that the epistemic gap of the unreliable narrator is not merely a clever structural parlor trick, but a profound philosophical exploration of human solipsism, illustrating how every consciousness constructs its own ornate, desperate mythology to ward off existential meaninglessness.</p>",
