    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>In Western philosophical aesthetics, descending from the Enlightenment treatises of Immanuel Kant and G.W.F. Hegel, a rigid hierarchical dichotomy has historically segregated &ldquo;fine art&rdquo; from &ldquo;craft.&rdquo; Fine art&mdash;epitomized by easel painting, monumental sculpture, and art music&mdash;was theorized as an autonomous, disinterested endeavor pursuing pure intellectual contemplation, elevated above mundane material utility. Kant&rsquo;s <i>Critique of Judgment</i> explicitly codified this distinction by stipulating that authentic aesthetic judgment requires complete detachment from practical function, sensory appetite, and economic interest. Conversely, functional objects, domestic textiles, ceramics, and wearable regalia were relegated to the subordinate taxonomy of &ldquo;craft&rdquo; or &ldquo;applied art,&rdquo; presumed to lack conceptual transcendence. When European colonial institutions encountered Indigenous material culture&mdash;such as Navajo (Din&eacute;) weaving, Haida totem carving, or Pueblo pottery&mdash;they reflexively projected this bifurcated taxonomy onto non-Western traditions. Indigenous creations were either demoted to utilitarian ethnographic artifacts in natural history cabinets or, later, selectively aestheticized as primitive precursors to Western modernist abstraction, stripping them of their intrinsic philosophical epistemologies.</p>\n<p>Contemporary Indigenous scholars and decolonial art theorists, including Jolene Rickard and Dylan Robinson, have mounted a trenchant critique of this institutional legacy, articulating the counter-hegemonic framework of <i>visual sovereignty</i>. Under Indigenous ontological paradigms, the demarcation between aesthetic contemplation and functional utility is not merely artificial; it is conceptually incoherent. In Din&eacute; weaving traditions, for example, the intricate geometric serrations and rhythmic symmetries of a textile are never merely decorative flourishes applied to a passive surface. Rather, weaving is an embodied ceremonial practice encoding <i>H&oacute;zh&oacute;</i>&mdash;a foundational Din&eacute; philosophical concept encompassing harmony, cosmic order, beauty, and systemic balance. The physical acts of spinning wool, harvesting local vegetable dyes, and reciting prayers across the loom integrate the weaver into an active ecological and ancestral relationship, rendering the completed textile an animate manifestation of dynamic relationality.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, Indigenous aesthetic systems fundamentally resist the Western bourgeois paradigm of the static, commodified art object destined for passive optical consumption within a sterile gallery. Traditional material culture is frequently inseparable from oral storytelling, song, ceremonial choreography, and communal reciprocal obligations. A carved ritual mask or a painted hide is not an inert object designed to be contemplated across a detached aesthetic distance; it is an active spiritual participant within living communal ceremonies. Indeed, many Indigenous ceremonial items possess an intended temporal lifecycle, created to decompose naturally or be dismantled following a rite. To display such sacred items pinned behind museum glass&mdash;permanently preserved yet severed from the seasonal rhythms, vocal invocations, and bodily movements that activate their social efficacy&mdash;constitutes a profound epistemic violence. Such static display transforms an animate relational event into a dead, taxonomized curiosity for tourist spectatorship.</p>\n<p>In recent decades, metropolitan art institutions have attempted to redress these historical exclusions by acquiring Indigenous works for permanent modern art galleries. Yet critical theorists caution that mere institutional incorporation, absent a radical epistemic restructuring, risks perpetuating colonial assimilation. When a curator hangs a sacred Din&eacute; textile on a pristine white gallery wall purely as a visual analogue to a Frank Stella minimalist canvas, the museum subtly absorbs Indigenous creativity into the universalizing teleology of Western formalism. Decolonial visual sovereignty insists upon a far more radical demand: recognizing the sovereign authority of Indigenous communities to define, interpret, and contextualize their cultural production on their own philosophical terms. True decolonization requires acknowledging that Indigenous aesthetic systems constitute self-determined, autonomous traditions whose ultimate meaning and legitimacy owe nothing to Western artistic validation.</p>",
