    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 4 (Questions 19&ndash;24)</div>\n<p>In his incendiary 1975 Chancellor&rsquo;s Lecture at the University of Massachusetts, Nigerian novelist Chinua Achebe delivered a searing polemic that permanently fractured Western literary canonization: &ldquo;An Image of Africa: Racism in Conrad&rsquo;s <i>Heart of Darkness</i>.&rdquo; Delivered to an audience of Western literary scholars who viewed Joseph Conrad as an unassailable titan of high modernist prose alongside James Joyce and Virginia Woolf, Achebe audaciously challenged the near-sacrosanct status of Conrad&rsquo;s 1899 novella. He argued that beneath its celebrated stylistic impressionism, psychological complexity, and brooding existential dread lies a deeply entrenched, dehumanizing racism. Achebe&rsquo;s critique was not directed merely at Conrad&rsquo;s personal Victorian prejudices, but rather at the underlying narrative architecture of the novella, which systematically reduces the African continent and its inhabitants to an exotic, pre-linguistic metaphysical backdrop against which European moral degeneration&mdash;epitomized by the spiritual disintegration of the imperial agent Kurtz&mdash;is dramatically staged.</p>\n<p>Central to Achebe&rsquo;s indictment is Conrad&rsquo;s systematic denial of linguistic and cultural agency to African people. Throughout <i>Heart of Darkness</i>, Africans are stripped of articulate human speech and complex interiority. They are denied ordinary syntax and dialogue, permitted only frenzied shrieks, rhythmic groans, or guttural fragments of broken pidgin, such as the famous utterance, &ldquo;Mistah Kurtz&mdash;he dead.&rdquo; Marlow&rsquo;s voyeuristic narrative gaze repeatedly reduces Congolese human beings to disembodied limbs, &ldquo;black shapes,&rdquo; or monstrous shadows crouching in the river mist, whose very humanity is treated as a scandalous, baffling revelation. By portraying Africa as the primeval antithesis of European rationality&mdash;a sinister realm of instinctual darkness, unrestrained savagery, and primeval terror&mdash;Conrad constructs what Achebe identifies as a colonial fantasy. In this racial economy, Africa possesses no autonomous history, political institutions, or civilizational integrity of its own; it functions purely as an externalized psychological mirror reflecting Europe&rsquo;s own suppressed primitive impulses, or as Marlow remarks, a disturbing reminder of what Europeans once were before the dawn of civilization.</p>\n<p>Western defenders of Conrad swiftly mobilized against Achebe&rsquo;s assault, deploying formalist and historical defenses. Critics such as Cedric Watts and Ian Watt argued that Achebe committed the intentional fallacy by naively conflating Conrad the author with his fictional narrator Marlow. Furthermore, they contended that Conrad was a pioneering anti-imperialist whose grim depiction of the Belgian Congo savagely exposed King Leopold II&rsquo;s brutal rubber exploitation. Achebe forcefully dismantled this defense, demonstrating that Conrad&rsquo;s formal ironies fail to subvert the core dehumanizing paradigm. An author, Achebe asserted, cannot mount a coherent moral critique of imperial exploitation while simultaneously depicting the colonized population as subhuman monsters unworthy of language. For Achebe, Conrad&rsquo;s aesthetic brilliance did not excuse his dehumanization; rather, it rendered the novella far more insidious by disguising racial contempt as universal metaphysical tragedy.</p>\n<p>Achebe&rsquo;s constructive literary response to this colonial erasure culminated in his own canonical masterpiece, <i>Things Fall Apart</i> (1958). Written intentionally in a nuanced, proverbs-infused Africanized English, the novel recovers the dignified civic, legal, and spiritual life of pre-colonial Igbo society. By giving his characters profound psychological depth, elaborate rhetorical eloquence, and complex moral dilemmas, Achebe restored the African linguistic agency that colonial discourse had suppressed. <i>Things Fall Apart</i> proved that African literature did not require European validation or exoticist patronizing. Achebe&rsquo;s double project&mdash;dismantling Conrad&rsquo;s imperialist myth and constructing an autonomous African literary realism&mdash;redefined post-colonial theory, insisting that genuine literary greatness cannot coexist with the casual artistic annihilation of another people&rsquo;s humanity.</p>",
