    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>In their monumental 1979 feminist critical treatise, <i>The Madwoman in the Attic: The Woman Writer and the Nineteenth-Century Literary Imagination</i>, Sandra Gilbert and Susan Gubar fundamentally transformed literary theory by interrogating the patriarchal metaphors governing Western authorship. Pointing to the pervasive cultural trope that historically equated literary creation with male biological generativity&mdash;a metaphorical tradition wherein the pen was conceptualized as a figurative phallus&mdash;Gilbert and Gubar argued that nineteenth-century literary culture defined the author as a patriarch, a father-creator whose authority over text mirrored divine sovereignty over creation. In this deeply gendered paradigm, female authorship was perceived not merely as an anomaly, but as a transgressive biological and psychological contradiction. Where Harold Bloom had famously theorized the male writer&rsquo;s &ldquo;anxiety of influence&rdquo;&mdash;an agonistic Oedipal struggle against formidable male poetic precursors&mdash;Gilbert and Gubar posited that female writers suffered from a far more radical affliction: the &ldquo;anxiety of authorship,&rdquo; a paralyzing dread that women were structurally incapable of generating autonomous literary art.</p>\n<p>This anxiety of authorship was born of profound cultural isolation. Denied an accessible lineage of female precursors and surrounded by male-authored representations that reduced women to rigid, polarized archetypes, the nineteenth-century woman writer was coerced into confronting the confining binary of the &ldquo;Angel in the House&rdquo; versus the &ldquo;Monster.&rdquo; Popularized by Coventry Patmore&rsquo;s Victorian poem, the Angel was celebrated as utterly selfless, submissive, pure, and devoid of personal desire or creative ego. Her demonic antithesis, the Monster, was the passionate, defiant, sexually autonomous woman who asserted her own will and was consequently pathologized as a grotesque, mad witch. To claim the authority of the pen, Gilbert and Gubar argue, female authors such as Charlotte Bront&euml;, Emily Dickinson, George Eliot, and Mary Shelley had first to struggle against both of these male-projected phantoms, recognizing that to accept either archetype meant submitting to artistic self-annihilation.</p>\n<p>To survive within this hostile patriarchal landscape without being dismissed as monstrous, nineteenth-century women writers developed a complex, subversive aesthetic strategy: the creation of palimpsestic, &ldquo;double-voiced&rdquo; texts. Beneath an ostensibly conventional surface narrative of domestic romance, pious sentimentality, and female resignation, these authors encoded dark, subversive undercurrents of female rage, frustration, and rebellion. The primary structural embodiment of this double vision is the titular &ldquo;madwoman in the attic&rdquo;&mdash;exemplified by Bertha Mason in Charlotte Bront&euml;&rsquo;s <i>Jane Eyre</i>. Rather than reading Bertha as an extraneous Gothic distraction or a mere impediment to Jane&rsquo;s romantic union with Rochester, Gilbert and Gubar interpret Bertha as Jane&rsquo;s violent psychological double: the ferocious manifestation of Jane&rsquo;s own unvoiced anger at patriarchal subjugation, setting fire to the tyrannical estate of Thornfield Hall.</p>\n<p>Gilbert and Gubar&rsquo;s paradigm galvanized feminist literary scholarship, establishing that nineteenth-century women&rsquo;s writing constituted a coherent, self-aware counter-tradition. However, subsequent post-colonial and intersectional theorists, most notably Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak in &ldquo;Three Women&rsquo;s Texts and a Critique of Imperialism,&rdquo; have challenged Gilbert and Gubar&rsquo;s Eurocentric blind spots. Spivak pointed out that treating Bertha Mason merely as a psychological metaphor for Jane Eyre&rsquo;s repressed domestic fury erases Bertha&rsquo;s concrete material reality as a colonized, Creole woman from Jamaica, whose sacrifice is violently required to facilitate the white English heroine&rsquo;s individualist emancipation. While this critique exposes the limitations of first-wave feminist universalism, Gilbert and Gubar&rsquo;s analysis remains foundational in demonstrating how nineteenth-century women writers forged an enduring aesthetic of subversion out of psychological imprisonment.</p>",
