    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 6 (Questions 31&ndash;33)</div>\n<p>In the rhetorical landscape of the eighteenth-century European Enlightenment, satire emerged as a preeminent weapon of philosophical inquiry and political critique. Yet literary theorists have long struggled to delineate the precise boundaries separating satire from its closely related rhetorical siblings, irony and parody. Irony operates fundamentally as an epistemic trope: a semantic tension between the literal assertion of words and their covert, intended meaning. Parody, by contrast, is primarily a formal mimicry: the exaggerated imitation of a specific genre, style, or authorial voice to expose its aesthetic absurdities. Satire is distinguishable from both by its normative moral imperative. While classical taxonomy divides satire into the gentle, amused ridicule of Horace and the fierce, bitter denunciation of Juvenal, both branches share an aggressive corrective purpose. Unlike parody, which may remain playful or purely stylistic, and unlike irony, which can function as detached philosophical skepticism, satire wields indignation and hyperbole to anatomize human folly or systemic vice with the explicit objective of moral reform.</p>\n<p>Nowhere is the volatile alchemy of these three modes more brilliantly synthesized than in Jonathan Swift&rsquo;s 1729 tract, <i>A Modest Proposal</i>. Having spent years publishing sincere, earnest political pamphlets advocating for economic reform in Ireland only to see them blithely dismissed by colonial administrators, Swift abandoned conventional polemic in favor of radical rhetorical subversion. Confronting the catastrophic famine, crushing poverty, and imperial exploitation devastating Ireland under British rule, Swift adopts the persona of a well-meaning, Enlightenment political arithmetician. On a formal level, the text functions as an impeccable parody of early modern utilitarian economics and demographic treatises, specifically mimicking the detached, quantitative prose style of Sir William Petty and the Royal Society. Swift&rsquo;s projector calculates with chilling mathematical precision the caloric yield of infant flesh, the market prices for roasted children, and the financial savings to absentee English landlords, all delivered in the dispassionate idiom of scientific philanthropy.</p>\n<p>It is through sustained structural irony that Swift&rsquo;s parody transforms into scathing Juvenalian satire. The shocking premise&mdash;that destitute Irish families should alleviate their poverty by fattening their one-year-old infants for slaughter and selling them as culinary delicacies to wealthy English elites&mdash;is never acknowledged as monstrous by the narrator. The moral horror is entirely generated within the cognitive gap between the projector&rsquo;s bland, rationalist equanimity and the reader&rsquo;s visceral moral repulsion. By adopting the very economic calculus that treated human beings as livestock or fungible capital, Swift exposes the monstrous presuppositions already governing British colonial policy. The rapacious English landlords, Swift bitterly implies, have already devoured Ireland&rsquo;s material resources, arable lands, and starving parents; consuming the children is merely the ultimate, merciless culmination of their predatory economic stewardship.</p>\n<p>Swift&rsquo;s masterstroke lies in his embedded catalog of authentic political remedies, tucked deceptively near the end of the tract under the guise of rejected expedients: taxing absentee landlords, consuming domestic manufactures, and cultivating civic solidarity. By having his absurd projector cavalierly dismiss these sensible, humane proposals as idle dreams incapable of practical implementation, Swift indicted both British tyranny and Irish apathy. While modern readers occasionally mistake the persona&rsquo;s cannibalistic scheme as a literal Gothic horror&mdash;a misunderstanding that plagued even eighteenth-century continental audiences&mdash;Swift&rsquo;s text endures as the quintessential triumph of satiric rhetoric, proving that when moral indignation is channeled through cold, rational parody, it can lay bare systemic atrocities that conventional polemics are powerless to disturb.</p>",
